icance if and only if there are essential truths characteristic of [p], D,\n604:essence or vice versa. And notice, moreover, that, if essence* is true, then,\n618:premises of the argument concerning significance and essence. We have\n630:to j; therefore, (iv) I ought to j. Suppose further that each of the premises\n631:and the conclusion are true. Dancy suggests that (i) gives you a reason to\n639:a putative counterexample to necessity.\n640:   The second and third potential counterexamples I adapt from Bricker\n666:   A fourth potential counterexample that I adapt from Schaffer (2010) is\n673:necessity is false, given that it’s not the case that the proposition\n699:counterexample, rather than mistaking a mere partial ground for a com-\n721:(2006), Chudnoff (manuscript), Dancy (2004, Ch. 3), Leuenberger (manuscript), Schaffer\n754:necessity we’re only interested in <p> as a contingent truth in any case, for if [p] is grounded\n773:significance. For suppose that [p] is grounded in D. There is obviously a possible rationally\n796:significance with respect to a connecting question is compatible with the question\n847:      In the final section of the article I consider potential counterexamples to necessity that\n848:perhaps can be recast as potential counterexamples to significance. There I argue that these\n859:involving cognitive significance form a substantive epistemic constraint on grounding. But\n874:This, however, doesn’t trivialize the essence constraint, for here the claim is that the con-\n1002:Leuenberger, S. (Manuscript). ‘Grounding and Necessity.’\n1026:Sider, T. (2001). Four-Dimensionalism. Oxford: Oxford University Press.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
