{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"reasoning","text":"**Considering schema requirements**\n\nI'm looking at the requirement that says not to invent canonical IDs beyond schema-allowed fields. It seems like `claim_key` isn't included in the schema but is part of the claim object's additional properties. The phrase \"beyond schema-allowed fields\" implies that perhaps `claim_id` is allowed there. This is a bit ambiguous. Non-normative shapes don't seem to have claim IDs, and existing deterministic validation resolves indexes to canonical claim IDs. I wonder about the validity of `source_dossier` references.\n**Clarifying quoting and paraphrasing rules**\n\nI need to remember that the schema requires a minimum of 40 characters. It's fine to include shorter snippets, each under 25 words, from the source to stay under a total of 90 words. The user expects concrete text, so I wonder if it's okay to paraphrase formal arguments with formulas. I should be cautious about copyright, limiting direct quotes to 25 words and constructing concise formal paraphrases with equations and labels for principles.\n**Examining formal argument structure**\n\nI think the formula seems likely okay. For significance, [p] is grounded in D only when connecting questions have cognitive significance. Lack of cognitive significance occurs when essential truths characterize [p], D, or related entities regarding ∧D → p.\n\nI've paraphrased the argument's formal chain, outlining its claims. It looks acceptable to use evidence excerpts in structured formats including small arrays if needed. I need to clarify relationships among claims and ensure the support structure is coherent—like checking if significance supports necessity and whether essential truths stem from the claims.\n**Structuring claims and relationships**\n\nI'm considering how to structure the claims and their relationships. For example, I can set up that claim \"2\" supports \"0,\" meaning significance supports the necessity thesis.\n\nClaim \"3\" can also support \"4,\" tying essence to the final argument. I might find it a bit odd that \"4\" is derived from \"2,\" but I can emit relationships even if they’re empty.\n\nIndexing my claims will help clarify the philosophical stakes, ensuring that everything, like counterexamples or objections, relates logically to the necessity thesis overall."}}
